  "notes": "Two houses and a shared pool along the edge of a slope falling to the west. From the west views (long bars of dark site stone, flush concrete roof, horizontal strip and square windows, the whole volume standing on a concrete floor slab that runs out past the stone with the supports recessed into shadow over the hillside), the corner and dusk views (a big roof slab thrown out over a glazed living room at the end of each house, a stone-parapet balcony cantilevered on the downhill corner, steps down to the ground), the gallery views (roof overhanging the east side over a timber deck, deep concrete beams running out east over the bedroom patios, carrying wires for climbers), the roof view (gravel roof inside a concrete upstand, U-shaped planter beams) and the pool view (the pool raised, stone-clad on a concrete base). The slope is a bank rising east to a level terrace; the living slab rises toward the east as the article describes. Which end is the living room, the second house's length (~14 m against ~24 m) and the pool sitting on the east terrace between them are read from the distant views and guessed; plans were not shown. Scrub is shown as trees and bushes; the pirca (dry-stone wall) below the houses is a straight run. Refinement pass (24 Sep 2026): the living roofs are now thin tilted slabs (shed with thick, 0.45 m, rising 0.5 m to the east) instead of solid wedges, with the gap over the stone walls closed by a concrete fillet. The hillside was too gentle to show the suspension: the east terrace is now 3 m up, the bank falls 3 m to the west over 17 m, and the houses (floor slab, stone, roofs, pool) were raised with it, so the concrete floor slab hovers about 1.5 m over the falling ground at the west edge with its supports set back in shadow. The ground is grass (the photos show a green meadow, not bare earth) with the bank in dry grass and pale granite boulders as in the corner view; the scrub trees are umbrella-crowned (espinillo, chañar), with three more on the lower ground and bushes on the bank. The stone is rubble (grey-brown field stone) and reads darker than the concrete slabs. Still guessed: the real fall of the ground (drawn as one even bank), the boulders' and trees' positions, the timber-slat soffit of the gallery (not drawn; it cannot be seen from above), and the steps down from the living-room corner. Refined 24 Sep 2026: the pirca is now a wall of pale rounded field stones in two courses, as the path views show (it was one flat dark block); the bedroom patios under the east beams are gravel squares with planted strips under the beams (the roof and patio views); concrete steps down the west side from the living-room corner (the dusk view). Refined 28 Oct 2026 (three rounds against the corner views 01 and 06, the gallery view 02, the roof view 05 and the west wall 00; photographs reachable): the five biggest mismatches, most important first: (1) the roofs, beams and slabs read cool near-white; the board-formed concrete in the photographs is warm grey-brown (about 117-130, 103-112, 87-95), so they are 'stone' now (the boulders stay 'concrete'); (2) the east frontage was a lush lawn; photos 02 and 05 show bare sandy earth with patchy tufts of grass, a green strip only along the deck, so the terrace is 'sand' with 70 tufts of grass and dry grass; (3) the shared pool was a bright blue; the article calls it \"a horizontal mirror\" that echoes the roof planes and no photograph shows its water (photo 04 shows only its stone-clad wall), so it is drawn dark ('slate'), a guess; (4) the stone is mottled with rust-brown and orange stones (photos 00, 01, 06): runs of 'sandstone' in the west faces of both houses; (5) the beams over the bedroom patios are U-shaped planters, green with climbers (photo 05), so a strip of plant rides on each. Still guessed: the real fall of the ground, the rust mottle's places, the second house's length, and the concrete's exact tone (the photographs show it mostly in shade, so the roofs may still read a little light). The dark and white lattice seen at the plate's foot under the raised ground at its edge is the renderer's (the plate's skirt), not a part of the model, and is left alone here."
